      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2\\sqrt{2}t,\\,4⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}⟨4t,\\,\\sqrt{2}(4-{t}^{2}),\\,-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(4t)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}(4-{t}^{2}))}^{2}+(-4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+2{(4-{t}^{2})}^{2}+16{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+(32-16{t}^{2}+2{t}^{4})+16{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{4}+16{t}^{2}+32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2({t}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+4})}{{t}^{2}+4}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
